/-
Johnson/Top/H/KeyRangesFull.lean: the two lookups of the key file at full size as written values,
and one named hypothesis: they pass the test on numbers.

Definitions, a statement with its proof, and a guard. Johnson/Top/H/KeyPairs.lean has one test,
`rangesB`, whose value true gives `RangesOK`.
Here the two lookups of the example at full size are written out: 24 parts, 812 and 396
runs of rows.
Main names:
  `lk0Full`, `lk1Full`: the two lookups (`WfLookup`, OnBytes/Params/Items.lean): tables of 2^8 and
    2^13 entries; offsets 128 and 4096; weights [1] and [1, 256]; twelve parts each, of 2^18 and of
    2^17 places; a run is ⟨firstPlace, firstRow, places, stride⟩.
  `HKeyRanges`: closed, without a parameter: `rangesB lk0Full lk1Full = true`.


Limits: where the two values come from: the repository's stored example set
prover/examples/target_full_f2. Three of its files were read: policy.txt, layout.txt and KL.bin
(settings, a layout, and scales); no key, leaf, proof, request or weight file was read. Lean's
evaluator ran the planner of the verifier that is written after the programs on these three
(`OnBytes.Model.Verify.planned`); the lookups of the item "ranges" of the key's first chain were
printed; a program wrote the print out as the two values. That they are that item's lookups is
stated by nothing in this file. The guard says that the test says true. A guard runs compiled code:
it is evidence by evaluation, no proof, so `HKeyRanges` stays a hypothesis here; `decide` does not
prove it: the reduction of the test gets stuck at the sorting `List.mergeSort`.
Johnson/Top/H/KeyRangesThm.lean shows what the hypothesis is to give (`RangesOK lk0Full lk1Full`)
clause by clause, by the kernel, so that the hypothesis is not needed. The limits of
Johnson/Top/H/KeyPortAt.lean and KeyPairs.lean hold.
-/
